Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) can efficiently solve complex problems that demand memory, error correction, and selection capabilities, mirroring brain circuit behaviors, albeit with limited applicability due to large training demands and memory needs. Brain structures pose constraints such as synaptic distance costs and thus inspired local masking, allowing task-independent predetermined graphs, even with sparsity rates as low as 4\%. We further refined this approach by introducing edge masking during training and utilising pre-built graphs during testing, resulting in considerable state storage reductions and fully applicable spatial convolution. We've implemented this model, named sRNN, and demonstrated its capabilities by successfully solving a wide range of cognitive tasks, outperforming task-specific and other model architectures, in both regular and brain-inspired sparse configurations. Notably, we've shown that with some modifications, sRNN can learn task sequences autonomously. We've also performed detailed analysis on how sRNN incorporates the learned graphs from regular training to regular task testing and found that to be the primary case. We've further verified that through the incorporation of noise to the model, we could replicate task-specific learning on new tasks. All code is openly accessible on GitHub. 

